-> HAS_FACET [node:4c740d82-541d-4c70-9970-793467c4f26e] @ 2026-09-08T13:31:04.312167+00:00
-> DEPENDS_ON [node:a815b136-0de2-49af-ac42-6192c12def24] @ 2026-09-08T13:31:04.312167+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:be297a51-6cb1-4526-bdb5-c01badf562a2] @ 2026-09-08T13:31:04.312167+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:c62c070b-e4a6-41b3-86f3-00ad5d96800f] @ 2026-09-08T13:31:04.312167+00:00

vidataflux is written out of the stack rather than waited for. The repository was empty, so adopting it meant blocking Faza 0 on a library that did not exist yet, for a role the project already has an answer to: data access is the SQLite layer behind a repository port, with the @logosdx adapters covering fetch, storage and events. Writing it out is the cheaper of the two honest answers and it costs little to reverse - the hexagonal port is exactly the seam a data library would slot into later, as an adapter, without disturbing the core. This clears the last blocker on Faza 0.
---
