-> ABOUT [node:49ba726c-031e-4a01-839f-11a76a8bf009] @ 2026-09-08T15:48:25.233911+00:00
-> DEPENDS_ON [node:77925b30-350a-42ee-a7de-7e8a023c5708] @ 2026-09-08T15:48:25.590140+00:00
-> DEPENDS_ON [node:79662ce5-7b55-458a-b0e9-f0efd406d5a8] @ 2026-09-08T15:48:25.478741+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:c62c070b-e4a6-41b3-86f3-00ad5d96800f] @ 2026-09-08T15:48:25.233911+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:e12ca220-5f82-4226-a2eb-9964701f03c4] @ 2026-09-08T15:48:25.233911+00:00

Storybook runs the web-components renderer rather than the Svelte one, because the components ship as custom elements and that is what the application loads. Rendering them in Storybook as internal Svelte components would exercise a form of the component the product never uses, and would stop catching the exact failure that has already happened once here: a build where the customElement option was silently dropped, so the element was never registered and the screen was blank while the build stayed green. Driving the registered element means a story that renders nothing is a story that has caught something real.
---
