path: /artifact/a-clock-injected-into-a-service-but-not
created_at: 2026-09-08T18:56:16.844498+00:00
needs_review: false
retrieval_weight: 1.0
trust_weight: 1.0
archived: false
-> HAS_FACET [node:4c740d82-541d-4c70-9970-793467c4f26e] @ 2026-09-08T18:56:16.844498+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:c62c070b-e4a6-41b3-86f3-00ad5d96800f] @ 2026-09-08T18:56:16.844498+00:00

A clock injected into a service but not into the repository underneath it measures age between two different clocks, and the result looks right in production and absurd in a test. The cache write takes the timestamp as a parameter rather than calling the wall clock itself, so the whole read-write-read cycle runs on one injected clock. This was found by a test asserting an age of 300 seconds and getting the distance between a fabricated date and the real present; the code would have shipped, because in production both clocks agree and nothing looks wrong.
---
