[node:e501e6f3-ae58-4154-a7db-f48d40b153e6]
type: constraint
tier: mid-term
path: /artifact/a-card-can-claim-a-whole-grid-column-an
created_at: 2026-09-09T06:22:37.677683+00:00
needs_review: false
retrieval_weight: 1.0
trust_weight: 1.0
archived: false
-> ABOUT [node:49ba726c-031e-4a01-839f-11a76a8bf009] @ 2026-09-09T06:22:37.677683+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:e12ca220-5f82-4226-a2eb-9964701f03c4] @ 2026-09-09T06:22:37.677683+00:00
-> ABOUT [node:e1da2600-04f7-4243-bb85-f79692e086ae] @ 2026-09-09T06:22:37.677683+00:00

A card can claim a whole grid column, and making that work needed two fixes that both fail silently. The layout contract carries span.rows and the renderer ignored it, so no file could ask for a full column however it was written; the guard was then a truthiness test, which skips zero, and zero is exactly the value chosen to mean the whole column. Worse, grid-row 1 / -1 counts against the explicit grid: with only grid-auto-rows the rows are implicit, -1 resolves to line 1, and the card asking for the column quietly receives a single cell with no error anywhere. Pages therefore template their rows explicitly, and the row count accounts for the span - a full-column card takes a column to itself, so the remaining cards share what is left, which is what decides the count rather than the raw card total.
---
[event:bed0bb8e-b502-4ff3-90e6-d9b6eaaea386]
