path: /artifact/rss-parser-keeps-content-encoded-und
created_at: 2026-09-09T12:12:18.080456+00:00
needs_review: false
retrieval_weight: 1.0
trust_weight: 1.0
archived: false
-> DEPENDS_ON [node:1dd47195-f438-4eb2-abe8-c16ac3cfcd4f] @ 2026-09-09T12:12:18.080456+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:4c740d82-541d-4c70-9970-793467c4f26e] @ 2026-09-09T12:12:18.080456+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:ccc18558-7181-44df-9608-de587d8d937c] @ 2026-09-09T12:12:18.080456+00:00

rss-parser keeps `<content:encoded>` under its own literal key (`item['content:encoded']`), separate from `.content`/`.contentSnippet` — for plain RSS 2.0 those instead come from `<description>` (verified against rss-parser's lib/parser.js: `xmlItem.description` maps to `.content`, while `content:encoded` is declared as its own field in lib/fields.js, not renamed). A comic/feed image extractor that only checks `.content` for embedded `<img>` tags will silently miss any feed that puts its real HTML body in `content:encoded` (common for WordPress-style feeds) and only a plain-text description elsewhere.
---
