[node:985e7b50-fce5-40da-9f30-ce40056370f2]
type: decision
tier: short-term
path: /artifact/the-audiometer-s-percent-display-is-rela
created_at: 2026-09-10T08:04:19.996355+00:00
needs_review: false
retrieval_weight: 1.0
trust_weight: 1.0
archived: false
-> HAS_FACET [node:c312eb94-94dd-4f25-a226-2ffe26dd6de7] @ 2026-09-10T08:04:19.996355+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:e12ca220-5f82-4226-a2eb-9964701f03c4] @ 2026-09-10T08:04:19.996355+00:00

The audiometer's percent display is relative to a trailing 30-second loudness window (REFERENCE_WINDOW_SECONDS), not a fixed absolute RMS-to-percent scale. The 100% reference is peak-hold-with-falloff: jumps up immediately to a new louder peak, eases down gradually (REFERENCE_FALLOFF_STEP=0.05 per sample) toward a newly-quieter window rather than snapping instantly, with a floor (MIN_REFERENCE_RMS=0.02) so a silent room's own noise floor never gets amplified toward 100%. This is what layout.schema.yaml's params.audiometer.unit description always claimed ("znormalizowane 0-100 względem ciszy/maksimum") but the original toPercent() implementation never actually did — it mapped raw RMS linearly to 0-100 on a fixed scale. A raw (unsmoothed) dB reading is now always shown as a secondary line regardless of the unit param.
---
