[node:009b7439-07c7-402f-ad22-664beda93a4d]
type: concept
tier: short-term
path: /artifact/k7-s-existing-fullscreen-mechanism-buil
created_at: 2026-09-14T19:47:35.142389+00:00
needs_review: false
retrieval_weight: 1.0
trust_weight: 1.0
archived: false
-> ABOUT [node:49ba726c-031e-4a01-839f-11a76a8bf009] @ 2026-09-14T19:47:35.142389+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:a1174884-8d33-4260-af5b-0f97264a2ea2] @ 2026-09-14T19:47:35.142389+00:00
-> DEPENDS_ON [node:c4b43952-3bbc-4917-87a7-9afbbebf1fd8] @ 2026-09-14T19:47:35.142389+00:00
-> DEPENDS_ON [node:ca0c4f4f-2eae-448f-a6bc-a8e70594e292] @ 2026-09-14T19:47:35.142389+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:e12ca220-5f82-4226-a2eb-9964701f03c4] @ 2026-09-14T19:47:35.142389+00:00

K7's existing fullscreen mechanism (built for the Slideshow, slideshow.ts + app.css) promotes the target element in place via CSS rather than reparenting it or using the browser Fullscreen API: a class (`k7-slideshow-active`, position:fixed !important; inset:0; z-index:500, plus a `k7-slideshow-veil` backdrop at z-index:499) is toggled directly on the widget's own custom-element host, which still works because Shadow DOM does not itself create a new CSS containing block — position:fixed on the host element resolves against the viewport unless an ancestor's own style (e.g. a transform) intervenes. The one real gotcha found and fixed here: `.pager-track`'s inline transform (used for page-swipe animation) would otherwise become that containing block, so it is cleared while fullscreen is active and restored via `pager.go()`'s saved value on exit. The promoted element also stays a DOM descendant of the pager's viewport even though it paints as fixed, so its touch/click events still bubble to the page-level pager underneath — Pager (pager.ts) gained `suspend()`/`resume()` specifically so an interaction meant to exit fullscreen isn't also read as a page swipe.
---
