Sixth correction to the k7-card-fullscreen plan, caught by a fifth independent /gw-plan-review pass on 2026-09-14. The plan had fullscreen-lock.ts's impure wiring "initialized once at boot" with a fixed {track, pager} snapshot. But main.ts's render() (called at initial boot, and again after every reconnect recovery and every pull-to-refresh -- an expected, frequent event on an always-on kitchen kiosk, not an edge case) already does deck.replaceChildren() and destroys+recreates both pager and slideshowController on every single call, specifically because the whole DOM subtree is rebuilt each time. A one-time init would leave fullscreen-lock.ts holding a detached track element, a destroyed Pager, and a destroyed SlideshowController after the very first reconnect: its pager.suspend()/resume() and transform clear/restore would silently act on dead objects with no effect on the live page, and its calls into the Slideshow's suspend()/resume() (the mechanism the previous two corrections built) would stop working entirely -- exactly the failure mode this project's own reconnect.ts and offline-shell work exists to guard against, just for a different subsystem. Fixed by making fullscreen-lock.ts a module-level singleton (matching main.ts's own existing idiom for pager/slideshowController: reassignable let bindings, not per-call construction) exposing a configure({track, pager, slideshow}) function that render() calls every time it finishes reassigning pager/slideshowController for that render. configure() also resets the pure {manualElId, slideshowElId} state to {null, null}, since the entire DOM was just replaced and any previously-tracked element no longer exists.
---
