-> HAS_FACET [node:e12ca220-5f82-4226-a2eb-9964701f03c4] @ 2026-09-15T06:33:57.045571+00:00

Real bug found only via real-browser testing during k7-card-fullscreen Phase 3 (not visible from code inspection, not caught by any typecheck/lint/unit test): binding a button's onclick reactively to `isFullscreen ? release : undefined` is wrong for a toggle button whose own mousedown/touchstart handler flips that same isFullscreen value. A real tap's mousedown fires first, synchronously flips the reactive value to true, Svelte's reactivity updates the onclick binding before the SAME tap's own click event fires (mousedown and click are separate browser-dispatched events with a microtask flush in between, even for one physical tap), so onclick resolves to release and the tap immediately undoes its own acquire. Reproduced with plain dispatchEvent(mousedown, mouseup, click) in headless Chrome and with a real CDP-injected touch tap. Fixed by using stable (non-reactively-swapped) handler functions with an explicit suppressClick flag: the pointerdown handler decides and acts (acquire), sets a flag; the click handler checks the flag and no-ops if set (already handled), otherwise treats a bare click (no preceding pointerdown) as a keyboard Enter/Space activation and toggles itself. touchstart additionally calls preventDefault() on the acquiring path only, to stop the browser synthesizing a trailing mousedown/click for that same touch (verified against real Chrome touch-to-mouse-event synthesis behavior, which only suppresses when preventDefault was called on touchstart/touchmove).
---
