-> HAS_FACET [node:e12ca220-5f82-4226-a2eb-9964701f03c4] @ 2026-09-15T06:40:46.717311+00:00

Change summary: k7-card-fullscreen added a reusable fullscreen affordance for K7 dashboard cards, shipped as an opt-in `fullscreen` prop on the shared Card.svelte shell (Calendar the first consumer), sharing its promotion mechanism with the existing Slideshow through a new module, fullscreen-lock.ts, rather than the native browser Fullscreen API (unreliable on this project's iPadOS 15 Safari baseline). Ownership between a card's manual button and the Slideshow's own idle-driven rotation is modeled as a pure reducer over {manualElId, slideshowElId} with a single derived promoted element -- manual always wins while held, and releasing falls through to whatever the Slideshow still has, which is how "closing manual fullscreen hands back to an already-fullscreen Slideshow, still rotating" falls out with no explicit branch. slideshow.ts gained its own suspend()/resume() pair (mirroring Pager's) so a manual session genuinely pauses the Slideshow's timers rather than just masking their visible effect.

Five independent /gw-plan-review passes found and fixed six real design gaps before any code was written: CSS promotion must target the custom-element host, not the shell's own shadow-DOM-local element (Shadow DOM style encapsulation rules this out entirely); the extraction must cover the Slideshow's rotate() as well as enter/exit; the button must acquire on touchstart/mousedown (not click) to win the race against the Slideshow's own window-level exit-on-interaction listener; the suspend/resume trigger must key off manualElId's own transitions, not the derived promotedElId (which doesn't cross null in the two scenarios that matter most); and the whole module must be re-pointed on every main.ts render() call, not constructed once at boot, since render() already tears down and rebuilds the Pager and Slideshow controller on every reconnect recovery and pull-to-refresh.

Two more bugs surfaced only through real-browser testing during implementation (headless Chrome via a transiently-installed, never-committed playwright-core), invisible to any static check: a reactively-swapped onclick handler that raced its own mousedown and undid its own tap's acquire; and a missing distinction between "is this card fullscreen at all" versus "does manual specifically own it," which let an acquiring tap on a card the Slideshow was already showing wrongly exit the Slideshow instead of taking control of it.

The recurring lesson across all eight findings: this codebase's DOM/event-ordering/lifecycle facts (Shadow DOM style scoping, touchstart-before-click ordering, main.ts's render() teardown-and-rebuild cycle, real touch-to-mouse-event synthesis rules) are load-bearing and easy to get wrong by inspection alone -- five rounds of independent review plus real-browser verification were what actually caught them, not any single pass.
---
