-> HAS_FACET [node:c62c070b-e4a6-41b3-86f3-00ad5d96800f] @ 2026-09-15T06:45:11.881469+00:00

k7-card-fullscreen's plan.md (Phase 1, "Extended test") committed to unit-testing SlideshowController.suspend()/resume()'s bookkeeping ("suspending while mode==='active' and resuming reschedules idle detection fresh; suspending while mode==='fullscreen' and resuming restarts the interval") but the shipped test/slideshow.test.ts contains no such tests — only shouldExitOnInteraction gained coverage. suspend()/resume() are part of slideshow.ts's impure DOM/timer-wiring half (real setTimeout/setInterval closures), which this project's own established convention already exempts from unit testing (the file's own header comment says so) — so the plan's "reducer/state level" framing for this specific test was not actually achievable without a bigger refactor (injectable fake timers), and the gap is arguably the right call, not a bug. But nothing in change.md's Outcome or the diff calls out that this specific promised test was dropped in favor of the Phase 3 real-browser scenarios (3/4/5) that do exercise the same behavior manually. Generalizable lesson: when a plan's "Extended test" section promises unit coverage for behavior that turns out to live in a module's already-declared-impure half, either the plan should be corrected before implementation closes, or the change's Outcome should explicitly note the substitution (automated → manual) so a reviewer isn't left to discover the gap by diffing plan.md against the test files.
---
