-> HAS_FACET [node:ccc18558-7181-44df-9608-de587d8d937c] @ 2026-09-15T07:14:16.880102+00:00

Decided 2026-09-15, reconciling with the lifetime decision that rejected .ics feeds (node c129f201: "a public .ics feed was rejected because it is read-only" -- made when .ics was being considered as the household's PRIMARY calendar, which needs bidirectional add/edit/delete): this is not a reversal of that decision, it is a narrower addition alongside it. The household's own Google Calendar keeps its existing bidirectional OAuth2 treatment untouched -- nothing about k7-multi-calendar changes it. Additional calendars (the motivating case: one .ics feed per kid's school schedule) are read-only by design, matching what .ics feeds actually are (no standard write-back protocol) and matching that school schedules were never going to be edited from K7 anyway. If a future need arises for K7 to edit an .ics-sourced calendar, that is a materially different, much larger feature (K7 would need to become the calendar's own host/mirror, not just a reader) and is explicitly out of scope here.
---
