-> ABOUT [node:3c7e1fe3-fba9-48d1-b1f3-8dc9b463a550] @ 2026-09-15T07:22:57.865159+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:4c740d82-541d-4c70-9970-793467c4f26e] @ 2026-09-15T07:22:57.865159+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:a1174884-8d33-4260-af5b-0f97264a2ea2] @ 2026-09-15T07:22:57.865159+00:00
-> DEPENDS_ON [node:c129f201-ae3c-45c8-89d7-a170bbaf5fad] @ 2026-09-15T07:22:57.865159+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:ccc18558-7181-44df-9608-de587d8d937c] @ 2026-09-15T07:22:57.865159+00:00

The calendar card has never had a real backend: K7Calendar.svelte fetches /api/calendar/week, which does not exist server-side at all (no route, no Google client file, no OAuth2 code anywhere in src/server) -- confirmed by grep across the whole server tree returning nothing. The card has always shown obviously-labelled mock events (usingMockData=true, state='idle', meta="dane przykladowe"), exactly as its own code comments say. "Google Calendar is integrated bidirectionally" (node c129f201) is a settled architectural DECISION, not a shipped fact -- k7-multi-calendar's .ics work does not need to avoid disturbing a working OAuth2 integration, because none exists to disturb. Building real Google OAuth2 is a separate, much larger, still-untouched piece of work (consent flow, token storage, Calendar API client) and stays out of scope here; the multi-calendar config shape should have a slot for a "google" source type so a future change can wire it in without another schema rework, but that source continues to behave exactly as it does today (mocked) until that happens.
---
