Decided 2026-09-15: k7-multi-calendar's Google Calendar work is bounded to the refresh-token exchange and a Calendar API read client, not a full OAuth2 web consent flow. The initial authorization (obtaining the refresh token) is a one-time, manual, out-of-band step the human performs themselves (via Google Cloud Console + OAuth playground or equivalent) and pastes into .env.local -- identical in shape to how the Cloudflare API token is handled for k7-lan-tls, and matching that GOOGLE_OAUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN is already a reserved config slot (src/server/config.ts, .env.schema) with no consent-flow code anywhere. K7's backend only needs: (1) a small token-refresh helper (POST to oauth2.googleapis.com/token with refresh_token+client_id+client_secret, standard OAuth2 refresh_token grant), (2) a Calendar API client (GET calendar/v3/calendars/{calendarId}/events, mapped to CalendarEvent[]), structurally similar to the existing open-meteo.ts fetcher. Needs two new config slots this change adds: GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID and GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET (obtained by the human from Google Cloud Console, same secret-handling discipline as every other credential in this project). This keeps the change bounded to one plan rather than requiring an epic split via /gw-slice.
---
