-> HAS_FACET [node:e12ca220-5f82-4226-a2eb-9964701f03c4] @ 2026-09-15T08:48:42.141442+00:00

Wireframe decision for k7-multi-calendar's degraded state: on a per-calendar fetch failure, fall back to that calendar's own last-good freshness-cache entry (reusing the existing createFreshnessService pattern exactly, no new backend mechanism) -- served with its age, per the no-placeholder-as-real rule. When that calendar has never successfully loaded at all (no cache entry exists), render a synthetic all-day CalendarEvent-shaped object per day (title "blad wczytywania"/"loading error", allDay:true) in that calendar's own bucket only -- reuses the existing event-rendering markup rather than a new error-banner component, and stays scoped to that one calendar's bucket so merging into the GLOWNY/main tab doesn't flood the whole week when only one of several calendars is broken. Per-calendar problem state surfaces on that calendar's own tab as a trailing glyph marker reusing Card.svelte's EXISTING state-glyph vocabulary exactly ([!] stale-from-cache, [X] no-cache-ever) -- no new iconography. The card's single footer badge (state/meta) reflects the worst state across all configured calendars. A stale-but-cached event is NOT visually distinguished from a fresh one within the event list itself (no per-event dimming) -- the tab marker and footer meta are the signal; dimming individual events in a merged multi-source view was judged as adding noise rather than clarity.
