k7-multi-calendar Phase 2 implementation findings (node-ical v0.27.2, verified against the installed library, not assumed): (1) node-ical's parseICS() is all-or-nothing per malformed RRULE field — an invalid FREQ or BYDAY value throws for the WHOLE file, not just that event; there is no per-event isolation at the library's own parse boundary. This means "one malformed event doesn't blank the feed" can only be guaranteed for malformed data that survives node-ical's own parse (e.g. an unparseable DTSTART value, which node-ical hands back as the literal string rather than a Date/throwing) — NOT for a structurally-invalid RRULE, which would take down the entire feed regardless of this module's own resilience. (2) node-ical parses a VALUE=DATE (all-day) field as LOCAL midnight of that calendar date, not UTC midnight (verified: this sandbox's TZ is Europe/Warsaw, and a DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260910 parsed to 2026-09-09T22:00:00.000Z, i.e. local midnight of the 10th) — so recovering the literal date string requires reading it back with LOCAL getters (getFullYear/getMonth/getDate), not UTC getters; this is symmetric regardless of what TZ the server process itself runs under, since both write and read sides use "local" consistently. Implemented in src/server/upstream/ics-calendar.ts's formatEventDate(). (3) icsCacheKey(calendarId) keys by the app-configured Calendar.id, not the feed URL — matches the plan's [node:53a1b84b] per-calendar-cache reasoning.
