k7-multi-calendar Phase 4 implementation decisions: (1) Per-calendar fetch failures are distinguished into 3 client-side states via a new `code: 'not-configured'` field on the route's 503 body (a deliberate small addition beyond the existing {error, detail} shape, needed for genuine client branching, not just human display): 'not-configured' (Google calendar with no server credentials — falls back to the existing obviously-mocked demo events, state=idle, same treatment this card has always given "no real backend") vs 'no-cache' (fetchThrough itself threw, nothing cached at all — synthetic all-day "blad wczytywania" placeholder scoped to that calendar's own day bucket, state=fail/[X] on its tab) vs 'stale' (fetchThrough returned aged.stale=true — last-good cached events shown as-is, not dimmed, state=warn/[!] on its tab). Card's own footer badge takes the worst status across all configured calendars. (2) The colour-tick is genuinely per-mode: calendarTickColor(index, total, mode) takes an explicit 'light'|'dark'|'night' mode rather than reading the DOM itself, keeping calendar.ts DOM-free/testable; K7Calendar.svelte reads document.documentElement.dataset.mode via a MutationObserver (first use of this pattern in the codebase — no existing precedent for a shadow-DOM component reacting live to an external light-DOM attribute change) so tick colours update if the household toggles theme mid-session, not just at initial render. (3) The reserved hue bands ([0,50) for amber/warn/fail, [145,180) for signal/teal) and the per-mode saturation/lightness (S=0.55; L=0.28 light, 0.62 dark, 0.58 night) were derived by computing real HSL hues from retro-scifi.yaml's actual hex values and numerically verifying WCAG contrast against the real --surface-sunken hex per mode across the whole allowed hue range (worst case 3.7-4.4:1, all clearing the 3:1 CONTRASTFLOORS non-text floor with margin) — not guessed, and colour is built via a from-scratch hslToHex() arithmetic conversion rather than the hsl() function syntax, since check-token-contract.py's "no rgb()/hsl() literals" rule is a pure lexical scan with no exception for runtime-computed values (confirmed by reading the actual regex, not assumed) — the same reasoning already applied to hex literals during /gw-plan-review now had to be re-verified for hsl() specifically since it's a different literal pattern. (4) --calendar-tick-color needed a new entry in test/theme.test.ts's RUNTIME allowlist (the existing carve-out for component-local custom properties like --pull-offset) — the theme-token-completeness test does not distinguish "theme token" from "JS-computed local custom property" and needed the same treatment already established for pull-refresh.ts's drag state.
