k7-multi-calendar Phase 5 complete: layout.yaml's kalendarz card migrated to the calendars array (one Google entry, showInMain:true, editable field removed), changelog.yaml entry added. Real-browser verification (transient playwright-core against real system Chrome, per this project's established precedent — installed and uninstalled, never committed) against a scratch 3-calendar layout confirmed all scenarios: (1) tab strip renders correctly with 4 tabs (GŁÓWNY + 3), active-tab brackets move correctly on click, no custom drag/snap logic needed beyond stopPropagation (native overflow-x scroll does the work, as the deck specified); (2) a real public .ics feed (Google's published US-holidays calendar, freely available) was fetched, parsed, and rendered correctly — verified Labor Day 2026-09-07 renders as a bare all-day date, and confirmed a genuinely empty week (Sept 14-21 2026, no US holidays that week) is NOT a bug, just real data; (3) Google-not-configured correctly falls back to the 4 mock events with meta="dane przykladowe"/badge="[--]" (idle), not the error-day treatment; (4) a deliberately-unreachable .ics URL correctly produces 7 synthetic all-day "blad wczytywania" placeholders scoped to that calendar's own tab, with badge="[X]" (fail) and meta="brak danych"; (5) confirmed the card's footer badge/meta aggregate worst-case across ALL configured calendars regardless of showInMain (the deck's own region label literally says "worst-case aggregation") — a calendar excluded from the merged main tab (showInMain:false) still drags the whole card's badge to fail if it's broken, which is the deck's explicit intended design, not a defect; (6) single-calendar regression case confirmed pixel-identical to pre-multi-calendar behavior: no .tabs element renders at all, mock-fallback meta/badge unchanged. No implementation bugs found during real-browser testing this time (unlike k7-card-fullscreen, which found 2 real bugs only via real-browser testing) — Phase 1-4's own typecheck/lint/test discipline plus the numeric contrast pre-verification caught everything static analysis could reasonably be expected to catch.
