-> HAS_FACET [node:c3dc31be-39b3-4422-a4f6-41136bfb48aa] @ 2026-09-15T12:48:47.622097+00:00

SSRF fix for /api/calendar/week (found live during k7-multi-calendar's home-server deploy, by the peer session running there): the route originally trusted client-supplied `mode`/`url`/`calendarId` query params and fetched `url` server-side with zero validation — a LAN client could direct K7's own server to fetch an arbitrary address (127.0.0.1, a docker bridge IP, another LAN host's admin panel like Portainer). Fix: the route now takes only `id` from the client and resolves the actual source (mode + url/calendarId) from layout.yaml server-side, via a new pure findConfiguredCalendar() in src/server/calendar-lookup.ts (extracted to its own module rather than left inline in index.ts, since index.ts boots the whole server as an import side effect and isn't unit-testable in isolation). The lookup searches recursively through grid cells and carousel slides, not just top-level page cards, since a calendar card could legitimately be nested per the layout schema's recursive-container shape — fixing only the top-level case would have just relocated the vulnerability to nested calendars. layout.yaml is trusted (only the household edits it); a request never is. Client-side, K7Calendar.svelte's calendarQuery() was simplified to send only `?id=`, dropping the now-unnecessary mode/url/calendarId params entirely.
---
